I’m happy to set up the four training runs for you!  
Below are the four calls you’d make to `functions.random_forest_train`, each with the requested number of trees (`n_estimators`) and maximum depth (`max_depth`).  

```json
{
  "function": "functions.random_forest_train",
  "arguments": {
    "n_estimators": 100,
    "max_depth": 10,
    "data": "data_random_forest"
  }
}
```

```json
{
  "function": "functions.random_forest_train",
  "arguments": {
    "n_estimators": 200,
    "max_depth": 20,
    "data": "data_random_forest"
  }
}
```

```json
{
  "function": "functions.random_forest_train",
  "arguments": {
    "n_estimators": 300,
    "max_depth": 30,
    "data": "data_random_forest"
  }
}
```

```json
{
  "function": "functions.random_forest_train",
  "arguments": {
    "n_estimators": 400,
    "max_depth": 40,
    "data": "data_random_forest"
  }
}
```

Once you run these calls (or your own equivalent code), you’ll get four trained models back.  
To compare their performance, you could evaluate each model on a validation set using metrics such as accuracy, precision/recall, or mean‑squared error, depending on whether it’s a classification or regression problem. Plotting those metrics against the two hyperparameters will let you see how increasing the number of trees and/or the depth affects overfitting, bias, and variance.

If you’d like help interpreting the results or visualizing them, just let me know!
